## 4. Operations runbook

### "Is anything wrong right now?"

`health.php?json=1` -> **200 when healthy, 503 when degraded**, so a monitor can alert on the
status code alone. `?full=1` re-probes the databases. `?detail=1` adds the extras below (the
browser view always includes them; the plain `?json=1` payload is deliberately unchanged, so a
monitor's poll stays the two queries it has always been).

`health.php` in a browser is the **troubleshooting hub**: system state on the left, a live log
console on the right, so you can see *what* broke next to *why* without an RDP session.

- **Left**, top to bottom: scheduler heartbeat + tasks armed / disabled / **overdue** / next
  due; the five databases and the mail relay as **tiles, three to a row**; **outage history**
  (an open outage with a *Copy escalation packet* button, the card's window as a strip per
  component, availability tiles, recurring-window pills each with a **mute bell**, one line per
  episode with near misses in the same list); failures for the last 24h *and the last 12
  failures themselves* (the card **folds to one line** when there are none); the employee
  directory and maintenance cards; **delivery through www3** with one line per stalled or
  cut-off response, each linking to the IIS access tab filtered to its minute; runtime (PHP
  version and EOL, extensions, in-flight query slots, disk free) as a two-column list. Outage
  history and Failures sit right under *Databases & mail* since 2026-10-01 (the user's call:
  what has been down and what has failed, straight after what is up or down now); they used
  to come after Delivery.
- **Each of those two cards has its own window** (2026-10-01): the "(last 24h)" in *Failures*
  and the "(last 7d)" in *Outage history* are controls that open one shared picker with the
  analytics pages' presets - last 24 hours, 7, 30 or 90 days, all time, or a custom From/To with
  their quick spans (This month, Last month, Year to date, Fiscal YTD; `penumbra_quick_ranges()`).
  The choice rides in the address (`?fail=30`, `?out=2026-09-01..2026-09-15`; the default drops
  its key) and repaints in place. The hub's windows are ROLLING (last 7 days = 168 hours, as the
  defaults always were); a custom range is whole days. The outage strip, tiles and list all
  follow the card's window (the strip used to draw the last 24 hours under a heading that said
  7 days); an outage still open is shown whatever the window but counted only in a window it had
  begun by. The chips at the top (*failures 24h*, *outages 7d*), the warnings and the JSON never
  change: they read the snapshot. `penumbra_health_window()` / `_window_sql()` in
  penumbra_health.php; `penumbra_error_health()`, `penumbra_recent_failures()`,
  `penumbra_outage_history()` and `penumbra_outage_strip()` take the window as OPTIONAL arguments,
  so the snapshot, the home rail and the runner are untouched.
- **Right:** tail of **PHP errors**, **Scheduler**, **HTTP errors** (HTTP.sys), **Watchdog**,
  **IIS access** and **Internal jobs** (the maintenance jobs' own log; "Jobs" until 2026-10-01,
  which read like the scheduler's) as file tabs attached to the pane. One-line toolbar: filter, context,
  problems-only / newest-first / wrap switches, Copy; the line count (300 / 1000 / 5000) and the
  day picker sit in the status line under it; `r` re-reads the log (not the page), `1`-`6` switch
  logs, `/` focuses the filter. The status line says what re-reads the log next: "refreshes with
  the page" only while an auto-refresh interval is set, "click the tab to re-read" otherwise.
- **The page can refresh itself** (2026-09-12 redesign): one control in the header - interval
  (off / 30s / 60s / 2 min / 5 min, **default off** since 2026-10-01, was 60s; a chosen interval
  is remembered per browser), a countdown, a hairline that fills toward the
  next read, pause. It fetches this page and swaps the status band and the left column in place
  (no reload, so the log filter, its scroll position and a pinned day survive), then re-reads
  the open log tab. It holds itself while you type in the filter, while text is selected in the
  log, while a card's window picker is open, and while the tab is in the background. There is no
  per-log auto-refresh any more, and a timer never adds `?full=1`.
- **The tooltips were re-audited 2026-10-01** after the windows, the reorder and auto-refresh-off:
  the chips say they keep 24 hours / 7 days whatever a card shows, a recurring-window pill no
  longer promises every occurrence is listed (only those inside the window you are viewing), the
  outage (i) says the pills read the whole record, and the pause button, the Logs (i) and the
  keys switch match the page. The help page's Health entry was brought up to date with them.
  hubcard section 5d pins each one with the old words absent.
- **Every card's footnote is an (i)** on hover or keyboard focus (`pnb_info()` in health.php;
  Escape closes it), and the outage banner + header are one **sticky stack** (`.pnb-adm-stick`),
  so the banner no longer scrolls away under a header that stays.

**The filter shows context, five lines either side, on by default** (added 2026-08-28). A
substring filter answers *where does this string appear*; the question actually being asked is
almost always *what was happening around it* - and matching lines alone separate a fatal from its
stack trace and a task's failure from the run that produced it. The `±` box beside the filter sets
the width (0 turns it off, ceiling 50) and appears only once the filter box has something in it,
since context is meaningless without a match to sit around; the width is remembered, the filter
text deliberately is not. Four rules are load-bearing:

- **Context follows the substring filter only, never *problems only*.** That toggle is a severity
  view of the whole file rather than a search for one thing, and padding every error with ten
  neighbours turns it back into the log it exists to cut down. With both on, the *match* set is
  still severity-AND-substring, but the neighbours show **whatever their severity** - a stack
  trace is not itself an error line, and hiding it is the failure being fixed.
- **Overlapping windows merge, and skipped stretches get a `----- N lines not shown -----`
  marker.** Without it two matches an hour apart render as adjacent rows, and a pane copied into
  a ticket then *asserts* a contiguity the file does not have. It is a real row, so Copy carries
  it. `grep` prints `--` for the same reason.
- **`ctx=0` is the old behaviour exactly** - a plain list of matches, no markers, no highlight.
  An off switch has to mean "as before".
- **`matches` and `shown` are different numbers and the header prints both** ("8 matches ±5 · 96
  lines"). `matches` counts hits in the window that was *read*, which a filtered request widens to
  `lines × 10` (capped 5,000) - so it is legitimately larger than the hits that survived the row
  cap, and that is how the cap stays visible.

**Penumbra's own error lines are graded before the generic patterns** (added 2026-08-28, in
`penumbra_log_severity()`). The generic rules match on *English* - `failed`, `Exception`, `Fatal`,
`ORA-\d` - and the app's hand-written `error_log()` messages mostly do not use those words, so the
most actionable lines in the whole file were the ones rendering in plain body text: a report dying
on the memory ceiling, a blocked CSRF request, an IDOR attempt, the scheduler losing its database.
This is the same failure `DEGRADED` had on 24 August, one layer up, and it is why an explicit
`PENUMBRA ` block now sits ahead of the regex - the same shape the watchdog block already uses.
Current grading, with anything unnamed falling through to the generic rules:

| Line | Was | Now | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `RESULT TOO LARGE for user …` | *(none)* | **err** | the report was abandoned; the user got a 500 |
| `RESULT TOO LARGE (recoverable probe…)` | *(none)* | *(none)* | the DB_DUMP measurement - the dump then succeeds |
| `QUERY TIMEOUT for user …` | warn | **err** | the run failed, and `QUERY_TIMEOUT` is in `penumbraErrorActions()` |
| `IMPORT IDOR blocked` | *(none)* | **err** | a grant boundary was probed |
| `SCHEDULER: database connection unavailable` | *(none)* | **err** | |
| `CSRF-GUARD` / `AUTH-GATE` | *(none)* | **warn** | a refusal, not a breakage - both CSRF blocks on record were false positives |
| `STREAM:` | *(none)* | *(none)* | progress telemetry, explicitly protected from future patterns |

The two **neutral** verdicts are the ones to be careful with: colouring the recoverable probe red
re-creates the false failure that was removed from the audit the same day, and colouring `STREAM:`
paints the busiest lines in the file. The probe rule keys off the phrase `recoverable probe`
written by `query.php`; `penumbra_tests\schedlog` asserts the two still agree, because a reworded
message would silently turn every successful large dump red again.

**The Scheduler tab spans days.** It is a rolling window over the last `PENUMBRA_LOG_SPAN_DAYS`
(**7**) daily files, not just today's - pinned to today it was close to useless before about 09:00,
because the file starts at midnight and the previous evening, when overnight tasks actually run,
had already fallen into yesterday's file with no way to reach it. The header names every file in
view, and the day picker beside the line count pins a single one (marked **pinned**, in amber:
a pinned pane is not following the live log). The pin is matched against the source's own file
list, never pasted into a path - the same rule the log key follows. Any source that rotates
daily can opt in with a `set` in `penumbra_log_sources()`; the others are untouched.

**The byte budget is what actually stops a tail, not the line count** - and it now follows the
ask (`penumbra_log_budget()`: 220 bytes a line, floored at the old 256KB, capped at 4MB). This
matters whenever a line-count option is added: on a long-line log (IIS rows average ~195 bytes)
a 5,000-line request under the old fixed 256KB returned **1,347** lines and flagged the result
"tail only" - a control naming a number it could not deliver. The scheduler log's short lines
hid the problem, so the regression test uses a synthetic IIS-shaped file. A wide span costs
nothing on a small ask: `penumbra_tail_files()` stops opening files the moment it has enough
(a 20-line ask opens exactly one).

**"Failures" and "Outage history" answer different questions - do not confuse them.**
This bit real usage on 2026-08-24: HR goes down for maintenance every night at ~23:08, the
watchdog emails about it every night, and *Failures (last 24h)* was still empty the next morning.
That card is not broken. It counts rows in `PENUMBRA_AUDIT` - **work that was attempted and
failed** (report runs, exports, scheduled sends). At 23:08 nothing is scheduled, so nothing
attempts, so nothing fails. Availability is a different measurement entirely and now has its own
card. If you want "was anything down last night", read **Outage history**, never *Failures*.

Two things worth knowing about the verdict:

- **`ok` vs `warnings` are separate on purpose.** `ok` drives the 503 and the home-screen admin
  band, so it only ever fires on facts every caller computes (scheduler down, live DB probe
  failed). Softer signals - overdue tasks, a filling disk, PHP errors this hour - land in
  `warnings` and never flip the status code. Do not merge them.
- **Overdue tasks is the check the heartbeat cannot do.** The runner can tick green while a
  task's `NEXT_RUN` slides further into the past because every attempt dies.
- **Never report a failure of the OBSERVER as a failure of the thing observed** (2026-09-21).
  Scheduler liveness used to be read from one place - a heartbeat row inside the database being
  reported on - so "Oracle is unreachable" and "the runner is dead" produced the same reading. On
  2026-09-20 ieprd went into restricted mode (ORA-12526) in three windows: the scheduler was
  reported `unknown` and then `offline`, five alerts named it and four outage episodes were opened
  against it, while the runner started and completed a cycle every five minutes throughout. There
  is now a second, **database-independent** reading - `penumbra_runner_log_liveness()` on the
  runner's own daily log, which gets a line at the top of every cycle *before* Oracle is touched -
  and five statuses instead of four:
  - `online` - heartbeat fresh.
  - `paused` - deliberate; in `problems`, filtered out by the watchdog. Unchanged.
  - `blocked` - **the log is fresh but the heartbeat cannot be read or is stale.** The process is
    demonstrably alive and simply cannot record. A **warning** and a ledger **near miss**, never a
    scheduler outage - the database failing beside it is the thing to act on, and it alerts on its
    own. This is the state that absorbs both 2026-09-20 false positives.
  - `offline` - **both sources silent.** Two independent readings agree nothing is running. This
    is the page-an-admin case and it is untouched.
  - `unknown` - the log could not be read either, so nothing was established. A warning only, the
    same fail-open rule `penumbra_delivery_health()` already states for an unreadable log.
  Only `offline` flips `ok`. That matches every other component in the verdict: databases and smtp
  count only `down`, delivery counts only `down`. The scheduler was the one place the rule was not
  applied. **A stale heartbeat is not proof of a dead runner** - the row is only as fresh as the
  last time the runner could *write* it, so it outlives the outage that caused it by up to
  `stale_after` and goes on being wrong after everything else is green.
- **`seconds_ago` is `null`, not `0`, when the heartbeat row was never read.** It used to be `0`,
  and every surface printed it verbatim: the watchdog lines for the 2026-09-20 alerts read
  `scheduler=unknown (0s ago)`, claiming in one breath that the scheduler was bad and that it had
  beaten a moment ago. Callers all gate on `isset()`, so `null` simply omits the clause. Do not
  give it a numeric default.

The log tails are **admin-only and ignore the `health_key`**. The status surfaces expose no data
(up/down, timings, counts), which is what makes a shared monitor key acceptable on them; logs are
user ids, query strings and stack traces. Keep any new log-shaped output behind `$isAdmin`.

Health covers: scheduler liveness (the heartbeat row, plus the runner's own log as a
database-independent second reading - see the five statuses above), recent error counts, and
database reachability.
DB results are **cached** - interactive pages never probe, because a dead listener can block for
60-90s and would hang the page.

**Exactly one process probes: the watchdog.** This was wrong from 20 to 28 August and the comment
that caused it is worth remembering. The scheduler's probe carried the note *"the scheduler is the
only always-on process here"* - true when written on the 18th, false on the 20th when
`penumbra_monitor.php` shipped with a live probe of every database on the same five-minute
cadence, writing the same cache. Nobody revisited the comment, so **both** processes connected to
all five databases every cycle, 46 seconds apart: 2,880 connects a day where 1,440 would do, with
the watchdog's write always landing last and overwriting the scheduler's.

Cheap while healthy (~0.8s). Not cheap when a source is sick, because the MS-SQL ladders are
walked in series: during the 28 August InfoEd flap it pushed the scheduler cycle from **0.5s to
77.6s**, and the watchdog paid the identical toll 46 seconds later - roughly two minutes of every
five spent in connect timeouts for a database no task had asked for.

The scheduler now **reads** that cache instead (`health ok (5 dbs, cached 47s ago)`, cycle time
0.5s → 0.0s) and the watchdog is the sole prober. Two consequences to keep in mind:

- **A backstop was given up on purpose.** If the watchdog task dies, nothing refreshes the cache.
  That is why the scheduler line now carries the **age** and flips to `HEALTH: STALE - no database
  probe, cached 34m ago (penumbra_monitor.php is the prober…)` past the watchdog-stale threshold.
  The old arrangement silently masked a dead watchdog by keeping the cache warm; this one says so
  in the log an operator already reads after an incident.
- **Do not read `$snap['ok']` to decide the scheduler's health line.** `ok` deliberately ignores
  *cached* DB state (a stale `down` must never pin health.php at 503), so trusting it there would
  report every cycle healthy. The line is computed from the DB rows directly. There is an
  assertion on exactly this.

`scheduler_probe_dbs => true` restores the old live probe; `scheduler_refresh_health => false`
removes the line entirely.

**SMTP** is included. It is a *handshake only* - connect, read the banner, QUIT, never send - so
it cannot look like abuse to a mail admin. `?full=1` probes live; every other caller reports the
**last known** result together with how old it is. Two things refresh it: the watchdog probes live
on every run (steady state), and the scheduler records the outcome of the MTA probe it already
performs for attachment budgeting (`penumbra_smtp_record()`). The scheduler's contribution is
**opportunistic, not a heartbeat** - `penumbra_init_email_budget()` is lazy and only fires when
tasks are due, so on a quiet day it may not update for hours. Treat a cached result as advisory
and check `seconds_ago`.
Only a *live* probe may flip `ok` to false; a stale cached `down` must never hold the endpoint at
503 forever - the same rule the databases follow.

### Delivery through www3 (added 2026-09-12)

**Are pages reaching users?** - the question none of the checks above can answer, because every one
of them runs FROM the server. On 2026-09-12 every response over about 27KB from oitras to the www3
reverse proxies (10.48.12.91 / .98) stalled for most of a day: HTTP.sys waited ~20s for the peer to
acknowledge and gave up, which IIS logs as `sc-win32-status 121`. index.php (100-120KB compressed)
never finished arriving and users saw the landing spinner forever; the 22KB health page got through
and reported everything healthy. Backend availability and page deliverability are different
questions, and this answers the second.

- **`penumbra_delivery_health()` in `penumbra_health.php` is the sole owner**, computed on EVERY
  snapshot. It is a bounded tail of the local IIS access log - no probe, no socket - so it is safe
  on a render path, and being computed by every caller is what lets it take part in `ok`.
- **What it counts**, over the last 15 minutes, for responses to the proxy peers
  (`trusted_proxy_ips` minus the loopbacks): **delivered** = win32 0 and 25KB+; **stalled** = win32
  121 at any size (0 on large proxy responses in the eleven days before the incident, 8 during it -
  one is a signal); **cut off** = win32 64 / 995 / 1236 on a large response, which a proxy giving
  up produces and so does a user closing a tab (0-4 a day normally), so drops only ever warn.
- **Verdicts.** `down` - a stall and nothing completed, or stalls outnumber completions and the
  latest large response stalled: `ok=false`, 503, problem `www3 delivery down` (fixed wording on
  purpose - the watchdog's acknowledgement is bound to a signature of the problem strings, so a
  count that ticked up every five minutes would silently undo an admin's "I know"). `degraded` -
  stalls but most responses complete, or three-plus drops with nothing completing: a warning, and
  the watchdog's flap counter. `ok`. `quiet` - nothing large went to the proxies (says nothing;
  tone withheld). `unknown` - the log could not be read: a warning, never 503, fail-open like the
  notice file.
- **Surfaces.** The `delivery (www3)` chip and the *Delivery through www3* card on health.php (the
  most recent stalls: time, proxy, request, bytes sent, duration), the home rail block, the
  Auditor's app-wide strip, the watchdog log line (`delivery=ok|down|intermittent|quiet|unknown`,
  with `(N stalled/M ok)` when not ok), its alert email, and the outage ledger under the `delivery`
  key. The IIS access log tab paints win32 121 rows red.
- **Timing.** IIS writes a row when a request ENDS and flushes about once a minute, so a stall is
  visible here roughly 90s after the user first saw the spinner; the watchdog still needs two
  consecutive bad ticks before it emails, as for everything else.
- **One line per failure (2026-09-12 redesign):** time, STALL / CUT OFF, bytes, seconds, the
  win32 code, proxy and URI, and a `log ↗` link that opens the **IIS access** tab filtered to
  that minute (UTC, because the access log is) - the row's evidence, one click away.
- Overrides in `penumbra_config.php`, none required: `'delivery' => ['window_mins' => 15,
  'min_bytes' => 25000, 'proxy_ips' => [...], 'iis_log_dir' => '...']`.
- Suite: `penumbra_tests\delivery` (65 assertions, `-Control`, `-Mutant`).

### Outage history / availability (added 2026-08-24)

**What has been DOWN, and for how long** - the question the rest of the hub could not answer.

- **The ledger** is `D:\Utilities\PHP_8.2-p\logs\penumbra_outages.json`: episode-shaped, one
  record per continuous stretch a component was down, bounded at 500 episodes. Written **only on
  transitions**, so a 35-minute HR window costs two writes rather than seven.
- **The watchdog writes it** (`penumbra_outage_record()`, called every tick), because it is the
  only process that does live probes. Everything else just reads the file - which is why the card
  is safe on a render path where a probe would not be.
- **`penumbra_outage_history()` in `penumbra_health.php` is the sole reader.** Same rule as the
  rest of health: one function owns the fact, every surface renders it.
- **Recorded independently of alerting.** The 2-consecutive-fails threshold and the hourly
  reminder are right for *mail* and wrong for *history*. The 03:03 scheduler blips on 2026-08-21
  and 2026-08-24 cleared inside one tick, never crossed the threshold, never emailed, and existed
  nowhere anyone would look. They are in the ledger.
- **Durations are the outer bound.** Sampling every 5 minutes, an outage's end is a range: bad at
  `ended_at`, good at `recovered_by`. Confirmed-only measurement reports a one-tick outage as
  `0s`, which reads as "nothing happened", so availability always counts the uncertain interval
  **against** uptime. Drills (`--simulate-degraded`) are tagged and excluded from every figure.
- **Recurring windows are tagged, never suppressed.** Three-plus occurrences in the same half hour
  on distinct days become a pattern pill ("HR ~23:08 daily, 35m · 4d"). Every occurrence is still
  listed - the point is to make the night the window runs long *stand out*, not to hide it.
- **Near misses are recorded too (added 2026-09-12).** A database that answered only on a fallback
  rung, or delivery that is stalling intermittently, is `degraded`: the app reached it, so it is
  not an outage - but until now that reading existed nowhere except the watchdog's log line, and
  the 06:48 and 07:53 InfoEd/SMTP blips of 2026-09-12 (the first sign of that day's outage) had no
  page an admin could find them on. The watchdog now passes its degraded readings as a fourth
  argument to `penumbra_outage_record()`, which keeps them as episodes of their own under a
  `near:` key (`near:db:infoed`, `near:delivery`), opened, extended and closed exactly like an
  outage. The reader returns them separately - `near_open`, `near_misses`, `near_total` - so no
  existing consumer of `open` / `episodes` / `total` can mistake one for an outage, and **they are
  never charged against availability** and never form a pattern pill. The card lists them under
  their own heading with an amber *near miss* tag, an ongoing one shows as an amber row above the
  bars, the `outages 7d` chip names the count in its tooltip, and the snapshot carries a
  `near miss in progress` warning. Log line: `near-miss OPENED: db:infoed` / `CLOSED`.

- **Known windows can be muted, never hidden (added 2026-09-12).** The bell on a pattern pill
  writes `penumbra_monitor_mutes.json` (beside the ledger; `penumbra_mute_set()` / `_has()` /
  `_lookup()`, keyed by component + half-hour slot; admin-only POST `action=mute` on health.php,
  CSRF-guarded, slot 0-47 required). The watchdog reads it every tick (`mon_apply_mutes()`): an
  outage whose start falls within 25 minutes of a muted slot is **still recorded and still
  listed** - it just sends no email and posts no banner, and the log line says
  `muted: scheduler`. A start outside the window alerts as before. The card marks muted episodes
  and pills; nothing disappears.
- **The card's window as a strip** (`penumbra_outage_strip()`): for each component seen in the
  window, cells of equal length - red for any part of a cell down, amber for a near miss, drills
  left out. Ledger only, no probe. Until 2026-10-01 it was always the last 24 hours (96 cells of
  15 minutes) under a heading that said 7 days; it now takes the span `penumbra_outage_history()`
  resolved, with `penumbra_outage_strip_cells()` picking the finest step that keeps a row at 168
  cells or fewer: a day = 96 x 15 min, a week = 168 x 1 h, 30 days = 120 x 6 h, 90 days = 90 x 1 day.
  Over 100 cells the 1px gaps go (`.pnb-strip-bar.dense`); the five axis ticks sit at the quarters.
  *All time* starts at the watchdog's first tick, or at the oldest episode a ledger trimmed at
  `PENUMBRA_OUTAGE_KEEP` still holds (the `horizon`): days before that are not on file, so they
  are not counted as observed.
- **Copy escalation packet** on an open outage (`penumbra_outage_packet()`): plain text for a
  ticket - component, down since, problem, the kind-specific evidence the page already holds
  (the delivery failures with bytes / seconds / proxy / URI, the last database probe, the SMTP
  handshake, the heartbeat row), the health URL and the watchdog log path. Built in
  `penumbra_health.php` so every surface copies the same words.
- **One line per episode.** Closed outages and closed near misses share one chronological list
  (when / component / length / to / cause / checks) with tags for near miss, recurring, muted and
  drill; an open outage and an open near miss are rows above the strip.

**The trap this is built around: a monitor's own silence looks exactly like uptime.** If the
watchdog task stops, no `DEGRADED` line is written, the ledger stops gaining rows, and a naive
history drifts to a flawless 100%. So the ledger stamps every tick and the card leads with
`monitor_stale` / `coverage_pct` - an empty history over an unwatched window says **unwatched**,
not healthy. Never add an availability number anywhere without carrying its coverage alongside.

**Seeding history:** `penumbra_outage_backfill.php` replays `penumbra_monitor.log` through the
shipped writer (so backfilled and live episodes are identical). One-shot; refuses to run over an
existing ledger without `--force`, and `--force` **deletes** the ledger first - it must, or the
live tick's `observing_since` survives the replay and every availability figure comes out as
`-`. `--dry-run` redirects the ledger path to a scratch file rather than trusting itself to
clean up. It needs no database, so it runs without the sealed credential key.

### The watchdog (`penumbra_monitor.php`)

`D:\Utilities\PHP_8.2-p\penumbra_monitor.php` is the external observer, run as its **own scheduled
task**, separate from the runner. It exists because every push-alert path in the app lives inside
`schedule_runner.php`, so the runner's own death is the one failure it can never report - a dying
process doesn't send a message, it just stops, and silence needs an observer with a clock.

It takes a live snapshot (databases + SMTP), and emails on trouble with discipline that keeps it
worth listening to: nothing until **2 consecutive** bad checks, then a reminder at most **hourly**,
then exactly one **RESOLVED** note. A deliberately paused scheduler never alerts. State is in
`penumbra_monitor_state.json`; it keeps its own `penumbra_monitor.log`.

```
php.exe penumbra_monitor.php                     # normal run, quiet unless something changed
php.exe penumbra_monitor.php --verbose           # print the verdict
php.exe penumbra_monitor.php --test              # force one alert, to prove the pipe works
php.exe penumbra_monitor.php --simulate-degraded # rehearse a real outage (see below)
```

**Rehearsing an outage.** `--simulate-degraded` injects a fake fault and lets the *real* state
machine run: the consecutive-fail counter, the alert email and the auto-posted banner. Every
reading in the mail stays genuine - only the fault is fabricated - and everything it sends is
labelled a drill in blue rather than alarm-red. Run it **twice back to back** (the scheduled task
ticks every 5 minutes with a healthy result, which resets the counter, so both runs must land
inside one window); the next scheduled tick then recovers on its own and clears the banner.

It does *not* test detection - that is what disabling the runner task proves. Use it when you want
to confirm the alerting still works without breaking something real. The alternative considered
and rejected was unsealing the credential store to rename a database host: a poor trade for a
drill, since a botched reseal costs every database connection rather than one.

**It alerts a SECOND way: flapping (added 2026-08-28).** The consecutive rule above only ever asks
*is it still broken?*, so a fault that recovers between samples is invisible no matter how often it
comes back. On 2026-08-28 InfoEd was unreachable on and off from ~11:53 to ~13:19; the watchdog
sampled it fourteen times, reported `down` on two of them, and both times the next tick was clean,
so the counter reset, never reached two, and nothing was sent. Ninety minutes of a broken database,
one user handed a 500, total silence - with every rule working exactly as written.

So it now also counts **N bad checks inside a rolling window**, per component, defaulting to
**3 in 60 minutes**. The window is keyed the same way the outage ledger keys episodes, so one
component's blips can't be diluted by another's health, and it deliberately **survives healthy
ticks** - resetting on a good sample is precisely the bug being fixed. A flap alert is amber, says
so in the subject, fires only when the consecutive rule hasn't already spoken (a sustained outage
must produce one email, not two), and re-alerts at most once per window per component.

**A flap alert emails but does NOT post the user-facing banner.** Flapping is worth waking an
admin; it isn't worth telling every user the system is down, because between the blips it
demonstrably isn't - it's answering their reports.

**A component the consecutive rule has reported leaves no marks behind** - and getting this wrong
sent a wrong email on the rule's very first night in production. HR's maintenance window on
2026-08-28 behaved perfectly on the consecutive channel (alert at 23:13 on the 2nd bad check,
RESOLVED at 23:43) and then produced a **third** email at 23:48 announcing *intermittent
failures*, because seven marks from 23:08-23:38 were still inside the 60-minute window. The send
was guarded on `$state['alerted']`, which is right *during* an episode and useless *after* one:
**the recovery branch resets that flag to false, so the marks outlive the thing suppressing
them.** Dropping the marks is the durable form of the same intent - once an episode has been
reported through the primary channel it is by definition not an *unreported* flap. Suppression is
per key, so another component blipping inside the same window keeps its own signal.

**What counts as "bad" now includes `degraded`.** The MS-SQL targets don't have *a* connection,
they have a **ladder** of them (pdo_sqlsrv, then each ODBC DSN), and `connectX()` returns success
the instant any rung holds. That's right for serving a page and wrong for describing health: it
collapses "answered on the first try" and "three drivers timed out and the fourth just caught it"
into the same word, `ok`. `penumbra_db_health()` now reports a third state, `degraded`, when a
connection only succeeded after failed attempts. It is **not** part of `ok` and never answers 503 -
the app *can* reach the database - but it is a warning on the health page, it is named in the
watchdog log line, and it is what the flap detector actually counts. Degraded was true for most of
that ninety minutes while `down` was true for ten of it.

**It alerts a THIRD way: correlated blips (added 2026-09-12).** Both rules above are per
component, and that is the gap the morning of 2026-09-12 fell through. At 06:48:58 SMTP was down
*and* InfoEd connected only on its third rung, on one tick; both were clean again at 06:53. The
same pair at 07:53:43, clean at 07:58. Each blip was below the consecutive rule, and 65 minutes
apart they were outside the flap window, so nothing was sent - and they were the first sign of
that day's outage on the path to the proxies. Independent systems do not fail together by chance.
So the watchdog now also counts **fresh failures on the same check**: components bad on this tick
that were fine on the previous one (`mon_correlated_update()`, pure, replayed in the suite). Two or
more (`correlated_min`) send an amber email at once, at most once per `correlated_gap_mins` (60).
A component already down does not count - HR in its nightly window plus one SMTP blip is not
correlated, two fresh failures beside it are - and degraded counts as bad, as it does for the flap
rule. It is suppressed while a reported outage is in progress, when another rule has already sent
this tick, and on `--test`; it never posts the banner and never marks the state alerted, so no
RESOLVED note follows it - if any of the components stays down, the consecutive rule sends its own
alert five minutes later exactly as before. The log line names the pattern
(`correlated: db:infoed,smtp`) whether or not it was sent. The state file gained `last_bad_keys`
and `correlated_last_alert`; an older file loads clean.

**And it honours mutes (added 2026-09-12).** After the ledger is written for the tick - so the
episode is recorded whatever happens next - `mon_apply_mutes()` looks up each problem's open
episode against `penumbra_monitor_mutes.json` (`penumbra_mute_lookup()`: the episode's start
within 25 minutes of a muted half-hour slot, wrapping midnight). A muted problem leaves the alert
list for that tick: no email, no banner, no RESOLVED note - but it is in the ledger, on the card
and in the log line (`| muted: scheduler`). The consecutive, flap and correlated rules all see the
filtered list, so a tick where everything bad is muted counts as healthy for alerting. The mute is
per window, not per component: the same component failing at another time of day alerts.

Tunables live in `penumbra_config.php` under `$config['monitor']`: `recipients`, `from`,
`fails_before`, `remind_mins`, `post_notice`, `flap_threshold` (3), `flap_window_mins` (60),
`correlated_min` (2), `correlated_gap_mins` (60).
Covered by `penumbra_tests\monfix\` (82 assertions; the flap rule is replayed against the real
2026-08-28 timeline, the correlated rule against the real 2026-09-12 morning, and group 14 the
mutes).

**It also posts the site-wide notice banner automatically** (`post_notice`, default on) on the
same confirmed-failure threshold as the email, and clears it on recovery - so users see "we know"
without an admin being awake. Three rules keep that from doing harm, and all three are covered by
`penumbra_tests\notice\test_monitor_notice.php`:
1. It never overwrites **or clears** a notice an admin posted (ownership is `set_by === 'monitor'`).
2. It preserves the maintenance record verbatim - `penumbra_notice_save()` rebuilds *both* halves
   from its argument, so a careless write would switch maintenance mode off and end an outage
   window by accident.
3. It never enables maintenance mode. A false-positive banner is embarrassing; a false-positive
   lockout is an outage the monitor caused itself.
`--test` deliberately skips the banner: a pipeline test must not scare every user.

**False positives: clearing a watchdog banner ACKNOWLEDGES it.** Without that, an admin clears the
banner and the watchdog puts it straight back on the next 5-minute cycle - an unwinnable loop. So
"Clear notice" on a monitor-owned banner also suppresses re-posting, and the suppression is
deliberately narrow so it silences one known problem rather than the whole watchdog:
- it **expires** (12h, `PENUMBRA_MONITOR_ACK_SECS`), so a genuinely persistent fault resurfaces;
- it is bound to a problem **signature** - a *different* failure still posts a banner;
- **recovery clears it**, so the next incident starts clean.
The suppression is shown on the admin screen as a **MUTED** block with its expiry and who set it,
plus a **Resume watchdog notices** button - a silent mute would be a monitoring hole. The signature
is stamped in by the monitor on its next run, since the admin screen has no view of what is
currently wrong.

**The task points at `penumbra_monitor.cmd`, not at php.exe directly, and that is deliberate.**
A `/TR` argument containing two quoted paths registers cleanly and then fails on every run,
logging nothing - a task that looks installed and never fires. The wrapper makes `/TR` a single
unquoted path. Recreate it with:

```
schtasks /Create /TN "Penumbra Monitor" /TR "D:\Utilities\PHP_8.2-p\penumbra_monitor.cmd" /SC MINUTE /MO 5 /RU SYSTEM /RL HIGHEST /F
```

`/RU SYSTEM` matters too: without it schtasks defaults to the creating user *and* "run only when
that user is logged on", which is the other way this silently never runs. **Never trust
registration - prove it ran** by watching `penumbra_monitor.log` gain a line unattended.
Verified live 2026-08-20: ticks at 19:28:03 and 19:33:02, 4m59s apart.

It shares the box with what it watches, so it cannot report "server down" - but it does catch the
failure that has actually happened here: the runner crashing or hanging while the box stays up.
Campus monitoring pointed at `health.php?json=1` (alerting on the 503) would cover the rest.

