### "A scheduled email didn't arrive"

1. Open `log\scheduler_YYYY-MM-DD.log` and find `Processing Task #<id>`.
2. **Look for `Security Check Passed`.** Its presence/absence is the fastest triage signal in the
   system: every task that logs it delivered; every task missing it failed the authorization step
   before the API call.
3. `Email sent to ...` confirms delivery. An `Error:` line gives the reason - and since 2026-08-18
   it reports what the server actually said rather than a canned guess.
4. Owners with "Email on failure" enabled are mailed automatically. If a failure produced *no*
   email, suspect a new error return missing `numeric_id` (see Landmines).
5. A `SKIPPED - missed window` line (paired with a `SCHEDULE_SKIPPED` audit row) is the task's
   own "Skip if missed by over an hour" option (Skip When card, added 2026-08-20) dropping an
   occurrence that the scheduler was down or paused across. That is configured behavior, not a
   failure - no failure email is sent by design, and the next delivery happens at the next
   regularly scheduled time.
6. A `First-run baseline check vs snapshot #N (...)` line followed by `skipped: Data unchanged
   since the baseline snapshot` (audit `SCHEDULE_SKIPPED`) is "Skip if no changes" honouring the
   hand-saved *Baseline (pre-scheduler)* snapshot on a task's FIRST run (2026-08-22). Until then
   the first run always went out: the signature gates only read scheduler-created signatures, and
   a browser-saved snapshot hashes differently, so the fallback is a row-level diff against the
   newest snapshot of any creator. Applies to Email, Snapshot, Drop-File and DB-Dump tasks.
7. A `CONDITION_EVAL_FAILED` audit row means the custom skip condition could not be evaluated and
   the delivery went ahead. Since 2026-08-22 a condition that resolves to `NaN` (row notes and
   toggles are browser-only) lands here too - it used to string-compare `NaN > 0` as TRUE and skip
   silently. The Auditor lists these under the Scheduler family chip.

### "The scheduler looks dead / the log stopped"

A healthy **idle** cycle is exactly **two** lines since 2026-08-24:

```
[2026-08-24 12:27:15] Starting Scheduler Run...
[2026-08-28 19:57:15] Run Complete: 0 tasks evaluated | health ok (5 dbs, cached 4m ago) | 0.0s
```

(The `cached Nm ago` half and the 0.0s arrived on 2026-08-28, when the scheduler stopped
re-probing the databases the watchdog already probes - see *Exactly one process probes* above.)

It used to be four - `Found 0 tasks due.` and `HEALTH: ok (...)` were separate lines, and at 288
cycles a day a measured **92%** of this log was those four lines repeating. They are folded into
the completion line, which also gained the elapsed time it never reported (watch that number: the
cycle budget is five minutes). Logs from before 2026-08-18 carry a fifth line,
`MTA advertises SIZE ...`; that probe is now lazy and only appears on cycles with due tasks, so
its absence in a recent log is normal and not a symptom.

**`Starting Scheduler Run...` deliberately stays on its own line**, and must not be folded in
later for another 50%. It is what makes a mid-cycle fatal visible: a start with no completion
after it is a crash, and if the pair were written as one line at the end, a dead run would log
*nothing at all* and be indistinguishable from a run that never began. Health also keeps its own
line whenever it is **not** ok - rarity is what makes it findable, and `DEGRADED` now colours as
an error in the console (it previously rendered in plain body text: the one line worth spotting
was the one line with no colour on it).

Grepping old logs still works - nothing was renamed, only merged. `Found N tasks due` appears in
files up to 2026-08-24; `Run Complete` spans both eras.

Three different causes, distinguishable by shape:

- **Log stops mid-task** -> the process died. Grep `php82_errors.log` for that timestamp; a PHP
  fatal bypasses every reporting path.
- **No cycle lines at all for a stretch, then normal cycles resume** -> it was paused
  deliberately. Since 2026-08-18 a pause writes `SCHEDULER PAUSED by ...` to the log; on older
  logs a pause left *no trace at all*.
- **Heartbeat stale (>330s) in health** -> genuinely not running. Check the Windows task.
  Since 2026-09-12 the runner stamps the heartbeat at the **start** of every cycle as well as
  the end (`schedule_runner.php`, right after "Starting Scheduler Run"), so a long task no longer
  reads as a dead runner: the recurring 03:03 "outages" in the ledger were the FI_EPA dump taking
  113 s while the heartbeat waited for the cycle to finish. A stale heartbeat now means no cycle
  *started*.

### "A report hangs or times out"

Oracle queries are capped at **120s** (`oci_set_call_timeout`), IIS FastCGI at 150s. A cancelled
query returns `error_code: timeout` and writes a `QUERY_TIMEOUT` audit row. Per-user concurrency
is capped (default 3) - exceeding it returns HTTP 429 `too_many_queries`, which is a guardrail,
not a fault.

**MS-SQL (InfoEd/PINS) has no reliable cancel** - 0 of 9 controlled trials cancelled - so those
are bounded only by the IIS 150s ceiling. The UI Stop button aborts the *browser request*; it does
not cancel the database statement.

### "A report fails only when the row limiter is OFF"

That is a **PHP memory** failure, not a SQL one - the query ran fine, there was just more of it
than PHP can hold. The limiter is nothing but a suffix (`query.php`, `if (!$noLimit && ...)`):
it appends `ORDER BY 1` + `FETCH FIRST 200 ROWS ONLY`. Turn it off and the whole result set is
buffered in one PHP array.

Since 2026-08-20 this returns `error_code: too_much_data`, a **"Too Much Data"** panel and a
`QUERY_TOOBIG` audit row. Before that the process died mid-fetch and - because `display_errors`
is Off - the browser got an empty HTTP 500 rendering as *"Error executing query: Internal Server
Error"*, indistinguishable from a database fault. **If you see that bare message in an old
report, check `php82_errors.log` for `Allowed memory size ... exhausted ... db_connection.php`.**

The ceiling is `penumbra_result_memory_budget_bytes()` = **60% of `memory_limit`**, checked every
128 rows in both fetch loops (OCI8 in `db_connection.php`, PDO in `query.php`). 60% because the
fetch array is *not* peak: the cell transform and `json_encode()` add ~37% on top while the array
is still alive. Override with `result_memory_budget_pct` in the config if ever needed.

**Capacity, measured 2026-08-20** on the widest real shape in the app - `SELECT A.*` from
STATS_COMBINED_V2 (79 columns) plus 4 derived = **83 columns**, ~12.6 KB/row buffered:

| `memory_limit` | guard trips at | peak at that size |
|---|---:|---:|
| 1024M (was) | ~48,000 rows | ~0.82 GB |
| 1536M | ~73,000 rows | ~1.23 GB |
| 2048M | ~97,000 rows | ~1.64 GB |
| 2560M | ~122,000 rows | ~2.05 GB |

Fewer columns scale it up almost linearly - the same budget holds roughly 7x as many rows at 12
columns. **Do not quote a row count without a column count**; rows alone mean nothing here.

**The server is NOT the binding constraint - the browser is, by about 12x.** Exports do not dodge
it either: `getExportData()` is client-side, so CSV/Excel/PDF are built from whatever the browser
already loaded.

**Measured 2026-08-20** (headless Edge, harness mirroring the real builder at `main.js` ~50658 -
`createElement` + `dataset.column` + `innerHTML` per cell, plus the per-row `masterRowData` Map).
Run-to-run spread at 83 columns was 1-3%:

| rows x cols | cells | JSON | parse | DOM build | + `rows=all` | **total blocking** |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 5,000 x 83 | 420k | 13 MB | 21 ms | 2.9 s | 4.0 s | **7.1 s** |
| 10,000 x 83 | 840k | 26 MB | 53 ms | 6.5 s | 8.6 s | **15.4 s** |
| 25,000 x 83 | 2.1M | 64 MB | 137 ms | 20.8 s | 22.5 s | **43.6 s** |
| 50,000 x 83 | 4.2M | 129 MB | 173 ms | 41.6 s | 92.0 s | **134 s** |
| 32,300 x 12 | 420k | 12 MB | 47 ms | 2.7 s | 4.4 s | **7.2 s** |
| 100,000 x 12 | 1.3M | 36 MB | 59 ms | 9.8 s | 13.6 s | **23.6 s** |

Three things fall out of this:

1. **Cost tracks CELLS, not rows** - 420k cells costs ~7.1 s whether that is 5,000x83 or
   32,300x12 (1% apart); 840k cells is 15.4 s vs 14.7 s. Budget ~**18 us per cell**, roughly
   linear to ~2M cells and worse above that. **Quote limits in cells.**
2. **`JSON.parse` is never the problem** (173 ms at 4.2M cells). Nor is the **JS heap** - but that
   is a trap, because `performance.memory` only sees the JS heap and **DOM nodes are not in it**.
   Measured by sampling the browser process tree instead (2026-08-20):

   | rows x 79 cols | DOM nodes | JS heap | **peak process RAM** |
   |---|---:|---:|---:|
   | 10,000 | 810k | 52 MB | **3.3 GB** |
   | 25,000 | 2.0M | 130 MB | **7.9 GB** |
   | 50,000 | 4.05M | 252 MB | **15.5 GB** |

   That is roughly **1-4 KB of real RAM per DOM node** and it scales linearly with cells. A user
   independently reported ~5.4 GB for 50,000 x 79 in a real browser, so treat the headless figures
   above as a pessimistic bound and "gigabytes, linear in cells" as the rule. **On a typical 8-16 GB
   laptop, memory bites before the clock does**: the tab swaps or dies partway through a render the
   time model says would merely be slow. Never size this from `performance.memory`.
3. **Pagination does not help.** It is CSS-only (`main.js` ~38905 toggles `style.display` on rows
   already in the DOM), so every row is built regardless. `rows=all` only adds the reveal+relayout
   - which is over half the total. Only row virtualization would move this ceiling.

**Users are warned before a long build** (2026-08-20). Above **250,000 cells** (~4.5 s) the loading
overlay switches to "Building the table: N rows by M columns. This takes about X seconds, and the
page will not respond until it finishes", plus a guidance line steering to fewer *rows* (and,
since hidden columns are built empty on 2026-09-30, fewer shown columns) - and
**#stopQueryBtn is disabled**, because once the build starts its click event only queues and can
never be handled. The estimate self-calibrates: `hideLoading()` already measures client render
cost (`lastLoadTime`, armed at response receipt), so a rolling median of us-per-cell is kept in
localStorage under `penumbraRenderRateSamples` and the 18 us/cell figure is only the seed.

The load-bearing part is the `await penumbraYieldToPaint()` immediately before the build loop:
without it the message is *set* but never *painted* - the build runs in the same task and the user
watches the stale "Executing Query" text through the entire freeze. That helper races `rAF`
against a 250 ms timeout on purpose, because `requestAnimationFrame` does not fire in a
backgrounded tab and a hung promise there would mean the table never renders at all.

**Working limit: ~500,000 cells** - about **6,000 rows at 83 columns**, or 40,000 at 12. That is a
~10 s freeze *and* roughly 0.7-1.9 GB of browser RAM, so both budgets land in the same place.

**Hidden columns (2026-09-30):** their cells are built EMPTY and filled on demand (section 5) -
~2 us a cell against ~18 us for a shown one, and one element with no content nodes - so a hidden
column costs about a tenth of a shown one. The loading notice counts it at
`_PNB_EMPTY_CELL_WEIGHT` = 0.1 (in the quoted estimate AND the recorded rate), and its author tip
now says hiding columns helps.

### Per-run size metrics

Every run of a **saved** report records its shape, so caps can be set from what reports actually
return rather than from guesswork:

- `PENUMBRA_AUDIT.ROW_COUNT / COL_COUNT / QUERY_MS` - one row per OPEN, i.e. the **distribution**.
  This is what you analyse.
- `PENUMBRA_REPORTS.LAST_ROW_COUNT / LAST_COL_COUNT / LAST_QUERY_MS / LAST_RUN_AT` - the **last run
  only**, denormalised so the repository can show a size with no correlated subquery per row.

Three surfaces, all live-verified 2026-08-20:

- **Repository details pane, Meta block** - "Last Run Size" / "Last Query Time", badged *(large)*
  over 250k cells and *(slow to display)* over 500k.
- **Repository table columns** (opt-in via List options): **Last Run Size**, **Last Query Time**,
  **Last Query Run**. Last Run Size **sorts by CELLS**, not rows - sorting it ranks reports by how heavy
  they actually are to render, which is the whole point.
  **THERE IS NO COLUMN CALLED JUST "Last Run", AND THERE MUST NEVER BE ONE AGAIN.** The timestamp
  column shipped as "Last Run" on 2026-08-20 while the repository already had a **Last Run** column
  (`LAST_RUN`, the newest fire across the report's scheduled tasks). Two rows with the same name in
  the List-options chooser, and two identical headers showing different dates when both were on.
  User-reported and fixed 2026-08-21 by qualifying **both**:
  - **Last Query Run** (`LAST_RUN_AT`) - moves only when query.php executes the report, i.e. someone
    OPENS it. Dash until the report is opened once after 2026-08-20, when the DB columns were added.
  - **Last Task Run** (`LAST_RUN`) - moves only when the SCHEDULER fires a task. Dash when the report
    has no tasks, or none have ever run.

  Both values are correct and they are SUPPOSED to disagree: `schedule_runner.php` never calls
  `penumbraRecordRunMetrics()`, so a scheduled send does not touch `LAST_RUN_AT`, and opening a
  report does not touch `LAST_RUN`. Renaming only the newcomer was not enough - a bare "Last Run"
  next to a "Last Query Run" still has to be told apart, so **every column name says which run it
  means**. Only labels changed; prefs are keyed by `key`, so no saved column layout broke.
  **The registry has no uniqueness check** - before adding a column, extract the labels between
  `var COLUMNS = [` and `var _colByKey` in `js/penumbra_repository.js` and run `sort | uniq -d`.
  Per-column help goes on the **chooser** row via the optional `desc` field, never on the `<th>`:
  that tooltip is `SORT_HINT`, and `applyServerReportSort` swaps and restores it.
- **Auditor → Events**, as a context chip on each OPEN row (`4,569×9 · 1.9s`). This costs **nothing
  extra**: `get_audit_log.php` selects the three columns from the audit row it is already reading -
  no second query, no join. OPENs recorded before the columns existed come back NULL and simply get
  no chip.
- **Report Metrics → "Run Size (rows)" KPI card** (`analytics.php`). Also free: the aggregates ride
  the same single scan as the existing counters. Headline is the **median**, not the last or the
  max - one freak run must not redefine what a report "is", and the median is what a cap would be
  set against. The sub-line always states the denominator ("median of 9 measured runs") so it never
  reads as settled fact on its first sample. Card appears only once there is something to measure.
- **Platform Metrics → Reports → "Heaviest Reports"** (`analytics_all.php`). Top 20 by **median
  cells**, which is the list of reports a cap would actually hit. **Windowed since 2026-09-23**
  (it used to be all-time under a "window not applied" note, and next to two cards that DID follow
  the range it read as broken): the medians are over the runs made in the selected range and
  weekday/hour scope. All time is the old catalogue-wide list, byte for byte. A range from before
  the size columns were added comes back empty, and the card says so rather than showing nothing.
  The same day, **Dead Reports** stopped listing reports created after a custom range's END - they
  had no views in the window only because they did not exist yet (13 of 52 for Aug 1 - Aug 15).
  Later that day both lists stopped ignoring filters silently: Heaviest reads **User / Department
  as who RAN the report** (the same reading Most-Opened gives them); Dead reads the Department
  filter as the **owner's** department, resolved from every report owner directly - the page's usual
  department user list only holds people active in the window, and a dead report's owner often
  is not. Heaviest still skips the visibility toggles (a run's size is not the runner's), and its
  header says so in a dashed "not applied" tag.

Recording happens at the very tail of `query.php`, after the payload is echoed, and it flushes
first so the user has their data before the two UPDATEs run. **That flush is gated on query.php
being the request** - flushing while `index.php` is capturing it corrupts the `?json=1` response.
See the landmine in section 5 before touching it.

Not built yet: **size over time** per report (is a report growing?). That is the one that predicts
future problems rather than describing today, but a trend chart needs history to be worth drawing.

**The columns are not created automatically, and the app CANNOT create them.** The application
account (`ieprd_db_user`, running with `CURRENT_SCHEMA = NC_Reporting`) is not the table owner and
has no ALTER privilege, so this is a **DBA task**. `health.php?ensure_metrics=1` still attempts the
ALTER - harmless, it fails and logs - but in practice its job here is as the **verifier**: run it
after the DBA and it reports `present` / `MISSING` for each table.

Hand the DBA this, to run **as the `NC_REPORTING` owner** (or anyone with `ALTER ANY TABLE`).
Nullable NUMBER columns with no DEFAULT are a metadata-only change in Oracle - no row rewrite, no
long lock - so this is safe on `PENUMBRA_AUDIT` at its full size:

```sql
ALTER TABLE NC_REPORTING.PENUMBRA_AUDIT   ADD (ROW_COUNT NUMBER, COL_COUNT NUMBER, QUERY_MS NUMBER);
ALTER TABLE NC_REPORTING.PENUMBRA_REPORTS ADD (LAST_ROW_COUNT NUMBER, LAST_COL_COUNT NUMBER,
                                               LAST_QUERY_MS NUMBER, LAST_RUN_AT TIMESTAMP);
```

**`UPDATE` on `PENUMBRA_AUDIT` IS granted - measured 2026-09-06, and this paragraph used to say the
opposite.** `ALL_TAB_PRIVS` lists exactly one grantee on the table, **`ORIED_RO`, holding `INSERT`,
`SELECT` and `UPDATE`**. Confirmed empirically as well as in the dictionary: **1,730 `OPEN` rows
carry a stamped `ROW_COUNT`**, the first at 2026-08-20 22:11 (when the columns were added) and the
latest the moment of measurement - so `penumbraRecordRunMetrics()`'s UPDATE lands on every run and
has since the feature shipped. Nothing needs granting. `PENUMBRA_REPORTS` needs nothing new either
(`set_report_owner.php` and `rename_report_short_id.php` already update it through the same
account). To re-check:

```sql
-- what the app account holds. NOTE: ALL_TAB_PRIVS on this database has TABLE_SCHEMA,
-- NOT an OWNER column - this query used to say OWNER and died with ORA-00904.
SELECT GRANTEE, PRIVILEGE FROM ALL_TAB_PRIVS
 WHERE TABLE_NAME = 'PENUMBRA_AUDIT' AND TABLE_SCHEMA = 'NC_REPORTING'
 ORDER BY GRANTEE, PRIVILEGE;

-- the empirical check, which also covers a privilege held through a role
SELECT COUNT(*) AS OPENS, COUNT(ROW_COUNT) AS STAMPED
  FROM NC_REPORTING.PENUMBRA_AUDIT WHERE ACTION = 'OPEN';
```

If the ALTER lands but the grant does not, the failure is **partial and silent by design**: the
`PENUMBRA_REPORTS` rollup (and therefore the repository display) still works, the audit-side
distribution does not, and `php82_errors.log` gets `PENUMBRA run-metrics write failed`. Grep for
that line after the first few runs.

Until that runs, **every read and write is guarded** by a static-cached `ALL_TAB_COLUMNS` check
(`penumbraAuditHasMetrics()` / `penumbraReportsHasMetrics()`), so an un-provisioned install simply
records nothing rather than failing report runs and repository lists with ORA-00904.

Two things to know if you touch this:

- The OPEN audit row is written **before** the query runs (deliberately - failure and timeout paths
  must still record an open), so the metrics are stamped on afterwards via `RETURNING ID` on the
  insert plus one UPDATE. `execClobInsert()`'s `$returnIdCol` is what makes that possible; it is
  additive, so the other 20 call sites are unaffected.
- The UPDATEs run **after** the response is echoed. Note this does not end the request under the
  IIS FastCGI SAPI (`fastcgi_finish_request` is php-fpm only), so they sit at the tail of the
  request - single-digit ms against a report measured in seconds. Failures are swallowed: a missing
  metric must never cost a user results they already have. Treat that as OPTIMISTIC: the harness does less per cell than the real builder (no
`formatCellContent`, resolver regex, search index or column properties) and it ran on a 20-core
workstation. The server guard at 1536M does not trip until ~73,000 rows at 83 columns, so for
interactive users **it will essentially never fire - they hit the frozen tab first.** The guard
protects the server; the 200-row limiter is what protects the user.

Two things that are easy to get wrong:
- `memory_limit` is **per request**, and the in-flight cap (3) is **per USER**, with the
  scheduler exempt - so nothing in the app bounds *global* concurrency. The FastCGI application
  for `PHP_8.2-p` sets no `maxInstances`, and the box registers five PHP pools shared with other
  sites. Size for (concurrent big reports) x peak, not for one.
- **oitras is an 8 GB / 2-vCPU VM** (checked 2026-08-20). That is what caps `memory_limit`, not
  the row math above: at 1536M three concurrent reports already peak near 3.7 GB before the OS,
  IIS, the scheduler and the other sites get their share. 2048M+ needs a VM RAM increase.
- `query.php` sets its own `memory_limit` (currently 1024M) at the top, overriding php.ini's
  256M. Change it **there**, not in php.ini. `index.php` repeats it for the `?json=1` API path
  and `schedule_runner.php` for scheduled runs - keep all three in step or a report will behave
  differently in email than in the browser.

