### "Hidden columns look wrong - build every cell up front again"

Since 2026-09-30 a hidden column's cells - and every cell of a row off the first page ("lazy
rows") - are built EMPTY and filled when something needs them (section 5). If a hidden column or
a page of rows ever shows blank cells, or an export, a sort, a group or a copy comes out wrong: create
an empty `js\penumbra_lazycells.off` in the app folder. The next page load builds every cell up
front again, exactly as before (index.php passes it to main.js as `penumbra_vars.lazyCellsOff`);
delete it to switch back. `&lazycells=off` does the same for one page - compare a report with
and without it first. The rest of that change (native results-wrapper lookups, the count after
the page loop, the skipped style resets) has no switch: it changes nothing a user can see.
`py -3 penumbra_tests\hiddencols\hc_patch.py reverse <in> <out>` takes the whole change back out
of main.js, index.php and README.md - exactly the inserted lines, so later deploys by others stay.

### "I need to take the app down"

`system_notice.php` (admin) posts a site-wide banner and/or enables maintenance mode.
Maintenance locks out non-admins (503 + `Retry-After`) and refuses report execution; **admins keep
full access and scheduled email keeps flowing** - pausing sends is the scheduler's separate
control. An expiry is mandatory and capped at 7 days, and every ambiguous state (missing/corrupt/
expired) resolves to *not* locking anyone out.

**Test the lockout with `&noadm`**, which makes an admin non-admin for that request. State lives
in a file, not the database, so the banner still renders during a database outage.

Downtime/pause interaction with tasks: on resume the scheduler services each missed task **once**
(oldest occurrence first since 2026-08-20), except tasks that opted into "Skip if missed by over
an hour" - those drop any occurrence the outage overlapped by more than an hour (audited as
`SCHEDULE_SKIPPED`). Pausing is therefore also a deliberate way to shed time-critical sends
during maintenance while catch-up tasks still deliver.

