### "Tag people for a report" / "who is in CALS_PREAWARD?"
Admin tools -> **Tags** (`people_tags.php`). A tag is a label on a person in the employee directory
(`PENUMBRA_PEOPLE_TAGS`: EMPLID, TAG, VAL, SOURCE, ADDED_BY, ADDED_AT, NOTE) and it exists only through
its members, so "start a tag" is opening a name and adding the first person. Names are upper-case
letters, digits and underscores (the table's CHECK constraint); what you type is upper-cased and
spaces become underscores. Add people from the directory search (tick, one POST) or paste a list of
Unity IDs / emails (unknowns are listed back, nothing else changes; someone who already carries the
tag keeps their row - the add is an idempotent MERGE). A value turns the tag into an attribute, a note
says why. Rename onto an existing name merges the two; Delete removes every membership after a
confirm. Every write is a `PENUMBRA_AUDIT` row (`PEOPLE_TAG_ADD` / `_REMOVE` / `_UPDATE` / `_RENAME`
/ `_DELETE`) and the page lists the last 40. In report SQL: `PENUMBRA_PEOPLE_V.TAGS LIKE '%|NAME|%'`,
`JSON_VALUE(ATTRS, '$.NAME')` for the value (a tag without one reads `1`), `N_TAGS` for the count;
the page shows both snippets with Copy buttons. Someone who leaves keeps their tags and shows as
inactive. The nightly refresh never touches the table.
**Group accounts (2026-09-25):** the same page registers addresses HR does not know - a Google Group, a
shared mailbox - as rows of `PENUMBRA_PEOPLE` with `SOURCE = 'MANUAL'`, `KIND = 'group'` or `'mailbox'` and
a synthetic `EMPLID` (`ACCT:<address>`), written on the WRITER connection (the app's own session only
reads the directory). Fields: address (any domain), display name, kind, units (2-, 4- or 6-digit OUC
prefixes, or `*` for campus-wide, stored in `OUCS_ALL` with the first one as `OUC`), a unit label
(`DEPT_NAME`), a description (`JOB_TITLE`) and, on creation, tags. Effects: the scheduler's picker
lists them with "(group)"; `{OUCS2D}`-style variables resolve their units, and `*` renders as a regex
group that matches every unit, so one filtered task can send each college its rows and a central
group everything; a tag finds them in any picker (the directory search matches tags too since
2026-09-25); `penumbraPeopleInOucs` / `DepartmentsUnder` and the hub's freshness count keep to HR
people (`KIND = 'person'` / `SOURCE = 'HR'`), and the sync's deactivate step and row guards skip
them, so a manual row survives every night. Retire keeps the row (tags and old recipient lists still
resolve). Audited as `PEOPLE_ACCOUNT_ADD` / `_UPDATE` / `_RETIRE` / `_RESTORE`. Report SQL that means
people should filter `KIND = 'person'`.

## 5. Landmines

Things that are non-obvious and have already caused real incidents.
- **PHP on oitras cannot spawn a process - from any SAPI.** `php.ini` line 323 disables
  `exec, passthru, shell_exec, system, proc_open, popen` (CLI included; there is no php-cli.ini),
  and `com_dotnet` is not loaded. That is why the maintenance jobs run INLINE inside the runner's
  cycle (after the end-of-cycle heartbeat, so the watchdog sees nothing as long as a job ends
  inside five minutes) and why a job must never call `exit()`. Proving a spawn recipe on a
  workstation proves nothing: the workstation's php.ini has no `disable_functions` (2026-09-23).
  Two more facts from the 2026-09-29 render spike: a CLI `-d disable_functions=` override DOES
  work with this php.ini (a policy decision, not taken), and **headless Edge refuses to run as
  SYSTEM** (exits in under 300 ms with no output, sandbox on or off) while it renders fine as
  NETWORK SERVICE or LOCAL SERVICE - which is why the render worker is its own task under
  NETWORK SERVICE and the runner (SYSTEM) never spawns anything.
- **The only way to execute something on oitras from a workstation is to piggyback an existing
  SYSTEM task wrapper** (WinRM, DCOM, remote schtasks are blocked; remotely created tasks never
  run). Recipe: a marker-gated `if exist ... (del marker & start "" /b payload.cmd)` block in
  `penumbra_monitor.cmd`, removed and hash-checked afterwards (scratch `spike_wrapper.pl`).
  `start /b` keeps the watchdog tick unblocked; the payload must bound itself.
- **`oci_bind_array_by_name()` binds PL/SQL collections, not SQL arrays.** A plain `INSERT ...
  VALUES (:col)` fed by it dies with ORA-01484 "arrays can only be bound to PL/SQL statements";
  the load has to be `BEGIN FORALL i IN 1 .. :n INSERT ... VALUES (:col(i), ...); END;`
  (people_sync stages 72 x 1,000 rows that way in ~5 s).
- **The webroot's `log\` folder is read-only to the web worker (IUSR), and in the web-writable
  state folder (`D:\Utilities\PHP_8.2-p\logs`) Users may create and write files but not delete
  them.** Anything a web page must be able to switch OFF therefore cannot be an "exists" flag:
  the maintenance control files are on unless they start with `off`, the tool writes `off`, and
  only the runner (SYSTEM) deletes. The runner's daily marker moved there with them; a marker
  left in `log\` is ignored.
- **HR's `PS_NC_ASSGN_EMP_VW.EMAIL_ADDR` is the SUPERVISOR's email, not the person's.** It sits
  right after `REPORTS_TO` in the view and equals the supervisor's `EMAILID` on 100% of active rows.
  It shipped in the directory as `EMAIL_ALIAS` for a few hours until the user read the data; it is
  `SUPERVISOR_EMAIL` now, and no resolver may match it as the person's own address (the peopledir
  suite pins that). The person's address is `EMAILID` (`PENUMBRA_PEOPLE.EMAIL`, the unityid form).
- **`penumbraToolPages()` and `penumbra_admin_tools()` are pinned by the toolvisit suite
  (exact key list, system-id count).** Adding an admin tool means both registries AND the suite's
  expectations (`penumbra_tests\toolvisit\run.php` sections 1 and 3) in the same change.
- **The health hub renders with NO database: every card reads the shared snapshot, so a fact that
  needs a query has to ride on the watchdog's cached probe (`penumbra_db_health()` and
  `penumbra_directory_health()` share `penumbra_health_cache.json`) or come from a file.** The
  hubcard suite renders each region over a hand-built `$snap` with only the lifted helpers loaded, so
  a card that calls anything else fails every region; it also pins six `pnb-tile` divs in the
  scheduler/databases region, so a new tile there is a suite change (the directory is a key/value
  card for that reason too). A cached verdict must be re-judged against NOW on read, or a watchdog
  that stops leaves it green forever.
- **A maintenance job's outcome is `maint_<job>.result` (JSON: finished, exit, secs, error, reason),
  written by the tick after every launch.** It is the only DB-free record of whether last night's
  job failed, and the hub warns on any non-zero exit the next morning. The exit codes are a contract
  every job must keep: 0 ok, 1 failed, 2 skipped by a guard, 3 locked (already running).


- **The admin chrome's notice bar + header are ONE sticky stack** (`.pnb-adm-stick`, emitted by
  `penumbra_admin_bar()` since 2026-09-12; the bar itself is `position: static` inside it).
  Anything that must clear the header has to clear the STACK, which is taller while a notice is
  posted: health.php measures it into `--pnb-stick-h` for its sticky log pane; the repo page's
  docked list measures the bar's bottom edge, which already includes the notice above it. A new
  sticky element with a hard-coded `top: 62px` will sit under the banner.
- **`penumbra_admin_open()` has a sixth argument, `$extra`:** trusted HTML placed between the
  titles and the nav (health.php's page-refresh control). Never pass request data through it.
- **A `hidden` attribute loses to any class that sets `display`.** The log toolbar's switches are
  `display: inline-flex` by class, so `#logCtxWrap hidden` stayed visible until
  `.pnb-tg[hidden] { display: none }` was added. Check the same whenever a component both sets
  display and is hidden by attribute.
- **The hub's `h2` flex rule is scoped to `.pnb-hub-grid .pnb-adm-card h2`.** Widening it to every
  `.pnb-adm-card` puts an 8px flex gap between the words of every other tool's headings.
- **The mute bell is useless without `data-slot`:** the POST handler refuses a missing or
  out-of-range slot (0-47). The hubcard suite's mutant deletes exactly that attribute and must go
  red.
- **Under `php -n` mbstring is not loaded** (a shared DLL here), so a suite that renders page
  code calling `mb_substr` needs a polyfill (hubcard has one) or the extension staged.

- **An `[IMPORT]` column keeps its own case everywhere, and the auto-quote tail must follow it -
  never the other way round.** Fixed 2026-09-10 (suite `importtrunc`, live-verified). Both emitters
  alias a column **verbatim** (`AS "award_id"`), but `resolveImportDataTags()`'s auto-quote tail
  used to rewrite outer references **unconditionally uppercased**, so the rewrite pointed at a name
  that did not exist: any column not already UPPERCASE died ORA-00904. Measured before the fix:
  `AWARD_ID` agreed; `award_id`, `AwardId` and `Award Id` all missed. `parseImportCsv()` keeps the
  header's original case (its `strtoupper` is duplicate detection only), so lowercase columns are
  ordinary, not exotic. It survived because it needs an **unqualified** reference - `SELECT *`
  never trips it and `b.DEPT_CODE` is skipped by the negative lookbehind for `.`.
  - **Do NOT "fix" this on the alias side.** Uppercasing the alias would make the reference resolve
    too, and would rename the output column of every working report that displays a lowercase
    import column - breaking pivot configs, saved widths and snippet references keyed on that name.
    The tail now maps `UPPER(name) => the column's actual stored case` and quotes with that, so an
    already-quoted reference in the wrong case is normalized to the column rather than to uppercase.
  - **A name arriving in two different cases is ambiguous and is deliberately left alone.** That
    needs two imports in one statement (within one import `parseImportCsv` dedupes on the uppercased
    header); there is no right answer, so those references are not rewritten and the author must
    qualify them.
- **The JSON row transport's payload is ONE temporary CLOB bind, and it must stay that way - the
  old chunked form cost more than everything else in the transport combined.** Changed 2026-09-10
  (`penumbraBuildOracleJsonRowset`, `queryViaOci8On`; live-verified on ieprd, HR and FIN). It used to split the payload
  into <=1000-byte VARCHAR2 binds joined with `||` in groups of 32, because a **string** bind over
  ~1333 bytes draws ORA-01460 on ieprd's CHAR-length-semantics sessions. Assembling that tree
  server-side was the real cost - measured before and after on ieprd:

  | rows | chunked binds | payload | execute (chunked) | execute (1 CLOB) | statement |
  |------|---------------|---------|-------------------|------------------|-----------|
  | 20,000 | 5,564 | 5.3 MB | 3.18 s | **0.19 s** | 73 KB -> **528 B** |
  | 50,000 | 13,939 | 13.3 MB | 13.40 s | **0.42 s** | 189 KB -> **528 B** |
  | 100,000 | 27,924 | 26.6 MB | 43.08 s | **0.83 s** | 390 KB -> **529 B** |

  Chunked grew about O(n^1.6) while *resolve* stayed linear - that gap is what identified the `||`
  tree rather than JSON_TABLE as the cost. The CLOB bind is linear, and the statement is now a flat
  ~528 bytes at any row count. A CLOB descriptor is not a string bind, so ORA-01460 never applied
  to it. Three things to know:
  - **The descriptor cannot be made where the SQL is built.** It must be created on the connection
    that will EXECUTE, and rows are resolved long before the outer statement is prepared. So the
    builder wraps the payload in `PenumbraClobBind` and `queryViaOci8On()` materializes, binds
    (`OCI_B_CLOB`) and frees it. Anything else in a bind pool stays a plain scalar.
  - **Bind the ARRAY ELEMENT, never a loop-local.** `oci_bind_by_name` keeps a reference to the
    variable it was handed; binding `$lob` and reassigning it next iteration repoints an
    already-bound placeholder. Same reason the scalar path binds `$refs[$name]`.
  - **Free the temporary LOBs at every exit** (`penumbraFreeTempLobs`, called beside all four
    `oci_free_statement` sites). The connection is `oci_connect`, not `oci_pconnect`, so a missed
    one dies with the request rather than accumulating in a worker - but a report holding several
    imports has no reason to sit on the temp tablespace. Verified flat across 10 consecutive runs.
  - `penumbraByteSafeChunks()` is left in place but is now **unused**; it encodes the ORA-01460
    knowledge if chunking is ever needed again.
- **`[IMPORT]` picks its SQL transport by SIZE, and the two lanes must stay interchangeable down
  to the column type.** Wired 2026-09-10 (`resolveImportDataTags()`, suite `importtrunc` 172/0).
  Under `PENUMBRA_IMPORT_JSON_MIN_BYTES` (512 KB of generated statement) an import inlines as
  `SELECT ... FROM dual UNION ALL` exactly as it always did; over it, the rows travel as chunked
  binds read by `JSON_TABLE`, which parses in constant time. Chosen by measurement, not by a tag
  flag, because unlike a subquery the server already holds the rows - there is nothing for the
  author to predict. **Verified live on ieprd 2026-09-10**, both lanes executed and compared cell
  by cell at 2,200 rows x 8 columns: identical result sets, zero diffs, literal 534,450 bytes /
  1.37 s vs JSON **4,631 bytes / 0.07 s**. At the full 20,000-row cap - the count the literal lane
  historically timed out on - the JSON lane returned in **1.309 s** (3,215 binds, 42 KB statement),
  with a numeric column summing exactly and skipping its nulls server-side. Four things will bite
  an editor here:
  - **The lanes must agree on every column's NAME and TYPE.** If they disagree, a report silently
    changes what a join means the moment it grows past the threshold - the worst possible failure
    for this feature. Both lanes read ONE `$emitAsNumber` map for exactly that reason, and the
    suite asserts name+type equality across all five storage shapes. A mutant that types the JSON
    lane from `$numericCols` instead is part of that suite.
  - **`$forceStringCol` is opt-OUT of NUMBER**, the inverse of this function's own default
    (string unless proven numeric). Getting it backwards types every text column NUMBER.
  - **Passing the numeric predicate is not enough to ship as a number.** The emitter drops quotes
    only when the STORED value is a PHP int/float - that is what separates a snapshot-sourced
    import (whole columns retyped by `penumbraImportRowsFromSnapshotRows`) from a CSV, whose cells
    are all strings and stay CHAR even when every one looks like a number. Deriving this properly
    also fixed a live **ORA-01790**: a CSV numeric-looking column with ANY blank used to emit
    `CAST(NULL AS NUMBER)` in one branch and `'1001'` in the next.
  - **The bind pool is SHARED with the subquery tags.** Build into a local array and merge only on
    success; clearing `$transportBinds` on failure would strand the CTE/SUBQUERY chunks already in
    it and the executor would die on ORA-01036 for binds no longer in the statement.

- **An oversize file in `imports/` used to be truncated AND the short copy saved, so the report
  answered with missing rows on every run and never said so.** Fixed 2026-09-10 (`query.php`,
  suite `importtrunc` 57/0). Both folder paths did `array_slice($parsedRows, 0, 20000)` and then
  wrote the result: the auto-refresh path UPDATEs the stored CLOB, the auto-create path INSERTs a
  new row. So the truncation was not one bad run - it was **persisted**, and because the folder is
  re-read on every execution a 25,000-row CSV stayed a 20,000-row report indefinitely. Nothing
  surfaced it: no error, no banner, and the audit line logged the *truncated* count as if it were
  the file's. `help_content.php` documented the behaviour as intended ("silently truncated to
  20,000"), which is how it survived. Three things to know before touching this code:
  - **Both guards record into `$importOversize` and skip; they do not throw.** Each site sits
    inside a `catch` that swallows exceptions - the refresh falls through to the stored copy, the
    auto-create only `error_log()`s and then the resolver blames a *missing* import for what is
    really an oversize file. A `throw` here is as invisible as the slice was. One gate before
    `resolveImportDataTags()` turns anything collected into a 400.
  - **`PENUMBRA_IMPORT_MAX_ROWS` exists so writers cannot drift, and three sites bypassed it with
    a bare `20000`** (both slices and the resolver's `$maxRows` argument). All now read the
    constant; the suite asserts `query.php` contains no bare `20000` anywhere.
  - **The same silent slice was live one layer down and is now fixed too** (2026-09-10, same
    suite): `resolveImportDataTags()` in `shared_logic.php` `array_slice`d to `$maxRows` directly
    above a byte check whose own comment reads *"Fail loud, never silently drop rows."* It now
    throws, which matters because it is the **last line of defence** - it catches an oversize row
    already sitting in `PENUMBRA_IMPORTS` (written before the cap existed, or by some future path
    that forgets to check), where no folder guard can help. Its `$maxRows` default is `null`
    resolving to `PENUMBRA_IMPORT_MAX_ROWS`, not a fourth copy of `20000`; the one remaining
    literal is the documented standalone fallback for callers that load `shared_logic.php`
    without `db_connection.php`.

- **Pivot RANK had three filter-blind defects, and they were in BOTH engines - which is why the
  pivot parity harness could never see them.** Fixed 2026-09-05; the lesson is the general one
  from that harness's own notes: *a differential test finds DRIFT, never a shared bug*, and a
  green diff over a rule that is wrong in both engines proves only that they are consistently
  wrong. All three lived in `pivot_apply_rank()` / the rank block of `generatePivotTable()`:
  1. **The rank VALUE ignored the aggregation's filter.** "Top 10 by Sum" on a pivot whose Sum is
     filtered ranked rows by their UNFILTERED total, so the leaderboard disagreed with the column
     it claimed to sort by. Measured on a 3-row fixture: Top 2 kept the row whose displayed value
     is second-to-LAST. Now `_rankFlt` / `$_rankFlt`.
  2. **The synthetic "(Other)" row dropped `_filteredBuckets`,** so `extractAggregationValue`
     found none and silently fell back to the unfiltered bucket: the rolled-up row printed a
     total under a column header that says it is filtered, and it did not add up to the Grand
     Total it was supposed to complete (measured live: 906 where 6 was correct, Grand Total now
     16 instead of a figure that could not be reconciled).
  3. **The same rollup dropped `wpairs`,** so `wavg@COL` on an "(Other)" row read **0** - exactly
     the failure `extractAggregationValue` documents at its own filtered-bucket read. Measured
     live: 4 where it used to be 0.
  The merge is now one helper per engine (`_rankMergeBucket` / `pivot_rank_merge_bucket`) instead
  of the two inline copies that had drifted; **touch the helper, never a call site.** Suite:
  `penumbra_tests\rankfilter` (23 assertions, THREE targeted mutants - one per defect, because
  each fix leaves the other two assertions passing on their own).
- **The pivot's "Hide empty rows and columns" option is ONE rule written twice, and both copies
  decide emptiness from BASE aggregations only.** `penumbraPivotAggValueIsEmpty` /
  `penumbraPivotEmptyKeySets` in `js/main.js` and `pivot_agg_value_is_empty` /
  `pivot_empty_key_sets` / `pivot_apply_hide_empty` in `schedule_runner.php`. If they disagree the
  emailed pivot shows a different grid than the screen and nothing errors - the same silent split
  that the hidden-grouping-column bug produced in group mode. **Touch the helper, never a call
  site**; the four PHP consumers (HTML, CSV, XLSX, `pivot_count_after_rank`) each call the applier
  in one line, on purpose. Three constraints hold the design together:
  - **Base aggregations only.** Every derived type (% change, running total, index, formula,
    `roll<N>`, ytd, yoy) is a function of the base numbers, so testing them too would be circular
    and would make the answer depend on column order. A pivot built *only* from derived
    aggregations therefore hides nothing - deliberate, not an oversight.
  - **Placement: after the keys are sorted, BEFORE `:fill` and BEFORE `pivot_apply_rank`.** Before
    fill because otherwise the sweep deletes the period gap fill just inserted; before rank so
    "Top 10" spends its slots on rows with data. Moving it after either one changes what is on
    screen.
  - **Display suppression, not a filter.** Grand Totals and every surviving cell must be
    byte-identical to the same pivot with the option off (verified live on `?id=int-col-mat`:
    272 body cells -> 140, zero surviving values changed). And it never blanks the whole grid -
    an all-zero pivot is a real answer; an empty one reads as a broken report.
  The two `.js` sentinels differ ON PURPOSE: JS tests `!isFinite` because its empty min/max
  sentinel is `Infinity`, PHP tests `+/-1e308` because that is the value *its* engine carries.
  Suite: `penumbra_tests\hideempty` (65 assertions, two targeted mutants).

- **`totalRowAggs: []` and `totalRowAggs: null` mean opposite things (2026-09-25).** `null` = no
  row-total override, one Grand Total cell per aggregation, as always. `[]` = an override with
  nothing left in it = **no Grand Total column at all** (the Grand Total *row* at the bottom stays).
  The builder writes `[]` when every value's box under Override Row Totals is cleared with its x, and
  the trigger spells it `hide` in the RowTotals position. JS gates every `.pivot-grand-total` emission
  on `_showRowTotalsCol`; the three runner builders gate on `$hasGrandTotalCol`. Mind that `[]` is
  truthy in JS and falsy in PHP - test it with `Array.isArray(x) && x.length === 0` /
  `is_array($x) && count($x) === 0`, never with a bare truthiness check. Ticking Override Row
  Totals prefills each box with that value's own aggregation and marks the set **pristine**
  (`data-rt-pristine` on `#pivot-row-total-slots`); a pristine set serializes as `null`, so ticking
  the box alone changes nothing. Suite: `penumbra_tests\fyreports`.
- **A combo LINE series stops at the last category it has rows for (2026-09-25).** Later
  categories are `null` (a gap to Chart.js), so the current fiscal year's running total ends at this
  month instead of running flat to June. Categories *between* real points still read 0 / hold the
  running total, and bars are untouched. Pareto takes its 100% from the last non-null point. And a
  series label may carry `{COLUMN}` tokens, resolved from the first row the series counts (the
  chart's first row when it has none) - only an author-written label, never the generated default.
- **A Grand Total slot must total its OWN value - its column, filter, unit and '$' (2026-09-25/26).**
