- **Deleting a report NULLs `PENUMBRA_AUDIT.REPORT_ID` on every one of that report's rows.**
  Proven live 2026-09-01: eight rows, all NULL the moment the report row went. Three
  consequences, none of them obvious from the code, and all three were live bugs:
  (1) `get_audit_data.php` reads `a.REPORT_ID`, so on the ONE path that needs it - restoring
  a delete - it could only ever hand the restore a **null `prior_report_id`**, which is why
  the undelete re-link silently never ran and journal notes, toggles and snapshots stayed
  stranded on the dead id. (2) The pre-delete version history is filed under **no id at
  all**, so `get_audit_log.php` (which keys on `REPORT_ID`) returned an EMPTY list for every
  restored report - the recovery destroyed the ability to recover further back.
  (3) Any "follow the lineage on read" fix is **inert**, because there is no old id to
  follow *to*. Fixed 2026-09-01 by writing the numeric id into the DELETE snapshot while it
  still exists (`delete_server_report.php`), preferring that over the nulled column
  (`get_audit_data.php`), and **re-pointing the orphaned rows at restore time** - scoped to
  `REPORT_ID IS NULL` and `ID <= source_audit_id`, matched on the ORIGINAL short id because
  an id conflict rewrites `$short_id` to `<id>_restored_<time>`. That re-point restores a
  foreign key the database itself cleared; it changes no evidence (BEFORE / AFTER / USER_ID
  / TIMESTAMP / SOURCE_DATA are untouched), so the append-only rule still holds.
  **Reports restored before this fix stay orphaned** - their rows carry neither an id nor a
  recoverable link. **None of this is reachable from an offline harness** - it took an
  end-to-end delete/restore against the real database to see it.
- **A restore writes the snapshot WHOLESALE; `?asof=` MERGES it over the live state.** Both pick
