- **`PENUMBRA_AUDIT.REPORT_ID` carries `FK_AUDIT_REPORT` -> `PENUMBRA_REPORTS` with a `SET NULL`
  delete rule (confirmed 2026-09-06), and it is the root of three live problems at once. DBA
  ticket: `ALTER TABLE NC_REPORTING.PENUMBRA_AUDIT DROP CONSTRAINT FK_AUDIT_REPORT;`**
  An audit table must not hold a foreign key to the thing it audits - it has to outlive it.
  Section 5 already records the NULLing as a landmine; this is the durable close. Measured
  2026-09-06: **4,598 of 27,642 audit rows (16.6%) are orphaned**, oldest 2025-11-15 and newest the
  day of measurement, so it is accumulating now rather than being a historical artifact. Only ~264
  of them - the snapshot-bearing `UPDATE` / `DELETE` / `CREATE` / `ROLLBACK` rows - carry a
  `$.short_id` that can link them back to a report at all; the other ~4,330 (`OPEN`=3508,
  `SCHEDULE_RUN_OK`=293, `DASH_OPEN`=178, ...) have no recoverable link by any means the app has.
  The symptoms:
  - **`purge_report_cli.php --deleted-older-than=N` is a SILENT NO-OP against the audit table.**
    Its only two id sources are `PENUMBRA_REPORTS.ID` (gone once the report is deleted) and
    `SELECT DISTINCT a.REPORT_ID ... WHERE a.REPORT_ID IS NOT NULL` (all NULLed by the FK), so
    `$p['ids']` comes back empty and the `foreach ($p['ids'] as $nid)` delete loop runs zero times.
    Verified live 2026-09-06 against the 13 reports deleted more than 90 days ago: every one
    printed `numeric ids: (none found)` and `TOTAL would delete = 0`. **The run still reports
    success**, which is what makes it dangerous - it reads like a working retention sweep. Do not
    rely on it until the FK is gone.
  - **A restored report regains its Version History but NOT its `OPEN` / `EXPORT` / `DENIED` /
    `JOURNAL` rows.** The re-point in `restore_server_report.php` matches on the snapshot, so only
    rows that carry one ever come back - despite the comment above it saying "every one of its
    rows". Those other rows stay orphaned permanently.
  - The re-point exists only to repair what the FK breaks. Dropping the constraint makes it
    vestigial rather than load-bearing.

  **Measured end to end 2026-09-06** with a throwaway report (`zz-relink-test`), driving the real
  `delete_server_report.php` and `restore_server_report.php`: 6 audit rows created (CREATE, 3x
  OPEN, UPDATE, EXPORT), delete blanked **all 7** including the DELETE row itself, and the restore
  reported *"3 earlier versions reconnected"* and came back under a **new id** (1389 -> 1421). Final
  tally: **4 of 8 rows carried an id; the 3 OPENs and the EXPORT are stranded permanently.** So 4b
  does work - that had been unknown - and it recovers only the snapshot-bearing rows, exactly as
  described above. Rows 27783-27791 are that test's residue and cannot be removed (see the DELETE
  privilege item below).
  **The post-drop path could NOT be tested**: setting `REPORT_ID` back onto those rows to simulate
  a dropped FK is itself refused by the FK - `ORA-02291: integrity constraint
  (NC_REPORTING.FK_AUDIT_REPORT) violated - parent key not found`. Which corroborates the
  constraint from Oracle's own mouth, and means **the only way to validate the post-drop behaviour
  is to drop it on a real schema.** The `$relinkTables` change that makes that safe is already in
  (2026-09-06) and provably inert until then - it matched 0 rows in this run, as designed.

  The constraint was read, not inferred: `ALL_CONSTRAINTS` returns exactly one relational
  constraint on the table, `FK_AUDIT_REPORT`, `DELETE_RULE = SET NULL`, referencing
  `SYS_C0023928` on `PENUMBRA_REPORTS`.

  **DO NOT DROP IT ON ITS OWN - a code change has to ship first or in the same window, or restore
  gets WORSE.** Today a restore re-points the snapshot-bearing rows via the `REPORT_ID IS NULL`
  match at `restore_server_report.php`, so a restored report gets a partial Version History. Drop
  the FK and those rows keep their OLD numeric id instead of going NULL; the re-point then matches
  nothing - and `get_audit_log.php` keys purely on `REPORT_ID`, so the restored report's Version
  History would come back **completely empty**. Partial loss becomes total loss. That is exactly
  what the 2026-09-06 change closed: `PENUMBRA_AUDIT` is now IN the old-to-new `$relinkTables`
  list, which today reads `PENUMBRA_JOURNAL`, `_SEEN`, `_TOGGLE`, `PENUMBRA_SNAPS`,
  `PENUMBRA_PRESETS` (added 2026-09-09) and `PENUMBRA_AUDIT`. **Keep that list in step with every
  table that keys on the numeric report id** - a table missing from it is silently stranded by any
  restore, which is how presets were missed for four days.
  Note the comment near `restore_server_report.php`'s RESTORE
  audit row claiming "get_audit_log.php follows prior_report_id" is **wrong**: `prior_report_id`
  has exactly one consumer in the codebase, `get_audit_data.php:198`, and the Auditor's list is
  not it.

  **The change that makes the drop safe is also the better fix, and it SHIPPED 2026-09-06:**
  `PENUMBRA_AUDIT` is in that `$relinkTables` old-to-new re-link, keyed on `prior_report_id` -
  already computed there, already guarded by `$prior_still_live`. It catches **every** one of
  the report's rows rather than only the snapshot-bearing ones, so it closes the second symptom
  above at the same time. Keep the `REPORT_ID IS NULL` re-point as a fallback for reports deleted
  *before* the FK goes, whose rows are already NULL.

  **The other real cost:** the FK is what makes Oracle reject an audit row naming a report that
  does not exist (ORA-02291). Without it a bug could write a bogus `REPORT_ID` silently, where
  today `logPenumbraAudit` would at least log the failure.
  **Trap:** `delete_server_report.php` *does* stash `report_id` inside the DELETE snapshot (the
  2026-09-01 fix), so the number is recoverable - but that alone does NOT fix the purge, because
  the rows themselves are `REPORT_ID IS NULL` and `DELETE ... WHERE REPORT_ID = 42` still matches
  nothing. Dropping the FK is the fix. The existing 4,598 stay orphaned either way; this stops the
  bleeding, it cannot rebuild links that were never recorded.
