- **A heatmap() colour lookup forced a style pass of the whole table per cell - CLOSED
  2026-10-01 (`heatperf`, suite `pagedisplay` G-heatbench).** `PenumbraHeat.colorizer()`'s
  per-value function called `readableOn(fill)` for every uncached ramp position, and that read
  the theme's text colour through `themeVar()` = `getComputedStyle(document.body)
  .getPropertyValue(...)`; between the heat pass's cell writes every such read forces a style
  recalculation of the whole table. Found by instrumenting a search on `?id=demosnippet` (1,001
  rows x 15 columns, two heatmap() rules): ~1 ms per coloured cell, 1.9 s of a 4.7 s render; a
  micro-benchmark in the live page: 2,000 lookups = 17 ms on a clean page, 1,000 lookups
  interleaved with cell writes = 8.4 s. The colorizer now reads the text colour ONCE (the theme
  cannot change while it lives; a theme switch re-renders), like `buildRamp()` reads the ramp
  endpoints; colours are unchanged (lazyrows / hiddencols differentials identical). Measured in the suite (heatbench: 1,001 rows, 200 a page, two heatmap() rules, a search keeping every row, headless Edge, back to back): live 1,868 computed-style reads and a 3.5 s render, the fix 14 reads and 0.15 s.
  The rule it illustrates, for any per-row pass: never read computed style between DOM writes.
  The rest of that render, measured the same way: the rewrite()/sparkline_cell() pass ~0.5 ms a
  row (SVG per row), the format() pass ~0.4 ms a row, resetCellStates' css resets ~0.2 ms a row -
  linear, not quadratic, left as they are.

